---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/linguistics/intermediate/syntax-trees-and-rules">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Syntax studies <strong>the hidden structure of sentences</strong> — not the word order you
    see, but the hierarchical tree beneath it. Its two greatest discoveries: sentences are
    nested units (hence ambiguity), and rules can move things (hence questions and passives).
    Grammar is the machinery no one is taught and everyone acquires.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="trees, ambiguity, recursion, movement" widget="equationStep" config={syntaxCfg} />

  <h2>Why syntax is evidence about the mind</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>You know rules you were never taught:</strong> "The cat seems sleeping" is wrong; "The cat seems to be sleeping" is right — no English class taught the distinction between seem + adjective and seem + infinitive. Everyone knows it anyway.tacit knowledge, made precise.</li>
    <li><strong>Universal patterns:</strong> all languages have subjects and predicates; verb-final languages tend to be case-marking; relative clauses nest similarly. Typology finds recurring constraints — evidence for design principles, if not Chomsky's exact ones.</li>
    <li><strong>The generative programme:</strong> Chomsky (1957→) claimed syntax is a formal system with universal principles and parameters — the most influential (and most contested) theory in the field (the expert lesson takes sides).</li>
    <li><strong>Parsing is how computers read:</strong> syntax trees power every grammar checker, translator and LLM's underlying structure — the computational-linguistics lesson shows the modern version.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Chomsky, Syntactic Structures (1957); Carnie, Syntax: A Generative Introduction; Pullum & Huddleston, Cambridge Grammar.</p>
</TopicPage>
